  "slug": "aging-senescence-a873",
  "summary": "Three preprints map liver senescence in situ as SenSpots, in human disease and aged mouse, while a p53 study tests a modulator of age-linked liver changes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is spatial resolution of cellular senescence in liver. Human spatial transcriptomics and scRNA-seq place senescent SenSpots at portal tracts and fibrotic septa, with phenotypes shifting across metabolic liver disease progression. Aged mouse liver multi-omics reports comparable inflammatory SenSpots with distinct epigenetic senotypes and zonation. A causal discovery approach applied to Visium data models causes and effects of senescence markers, which adds an inferential layer to descriptive maps. In aged mouse liver, p53 loss increases inflammation and metabolic damage, and MDM2 inhibitor-driven p53 activation reverses age-linked changes. Human omental adipose is a non-liver comparison, showing more senescence with age than obesity alone explains. Liver injury repair and myocarditis items are peripheral. All evidence is preprint-stage.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
